A marquise diamond eternity band is the most dramatic wedding band you can choose. The pointed stone silhouette catches light differently than any round or oval eternity band. It's also the most maintenance-intensive band style available.

The same pointed tips that create the visual impact are exactly where prong failures start. Most jewelers will tell you to bring any ring in every 12 months. For a marquise diamond eternity band, that schedule is not enough — you need every 6 months, and you need to know what a failing prong looks like before it fails.

This guide covers everything: the structural mechanics, all 5 failure points with timelines, eternity versus half-eternity, and how to match a marquise band to your engagement ring. See the full [marquise cut diamond guide](/marquise-cut-diamond) for shape fundamentals first.

---

## What Makes a Marquise Diamond Eternity Band Different

A standard round-prong eternity band uses rounded bead prongs. The contact points are smooth and curved. Metal-to-metal stress distributes evenly.

A marquise diamond eternity band is structurally different in three ways. The stones have pointed tips that require V-shaped prongs, not round beads. V-prongs have a much smaller cross-section at the tip.

There's less metal protecting the most vulnerable part of the stone. Adjacent stones sit very close together. The V-prongs of one stone's tip sit just millimeters from the V-prongs of the next stone's tip.

The eternity band structure eliminates the solid shank section found in a solitaire. Every flex force travels directly through the prong-and-stone structure. This combination — thin V-prongs, close stone spacing, no flex absorption — is what makes a marquise eternity band both beautiful and demanding.

The [marquise diamond length-to-width ratio guide](/marquise-diamond-length-to-width-ratio) explains how stone proportions directly affect prong spacing and V-tip clearance between adjacent stones.

