## The Alternating V-Tip Principle

Every marquise diamond in an eternity band needs two V-prongs — one at each pointed tip. In a correctly set band, the V-tips of adjacent stones never point directly at each other. Stone A's right-tip prong points slightly up and right; Stone B's left-tip prong points slightly down and left. This offset is the Alternating V-Tip Principle.

When the alternating orientation is correct, the V-tips of adjacent prongs approach each other at an angle. They don't press face-to-face. When a band is set incorrectly — or when prongs drift over time — the tips make direct contact.

Every flex of the band then grinds those tips against each other. The prong tip metal is only 0.3–0.5mm thick. It takes roughly 18–24 months of daily wear for a directly-contacting V-prong to thin to the failure point. A correctly offset V-prong lasts 5–7 years before needing retipping.

**How to check your band:** Inspect the prong tips of two adjacent stones under a 10× loupe. The tips should appear to point away from each other. If you see bright, mirror-polished metal on the inner face of any prong tip, the prongs are in contact and wearing down.

**What to ask at retail:** Ask specifically: "How are the V-prongs oriented on adjacent stones?" A confident answer — "alternating, so the tips offset" — tells you the setter understands the structure. A vague answer is a warning sign.

The [marquise cut diamond ideal proportions guide](/marquise-cut-diamond-ideal-proportions) explains how stone proportions affect the gap between adjacent V-prongs and minimum safe clearance.

