## 5 Prong Issues Marquise Diamond Eternity Band Owners Face at Year 3

These five issues follow a predictable timeline on marquise eternity bands worn daily. Some connect directly to the Alternating V-Tip Principle. Others come from the structural mechanics of the continuous eternity band format.

### Issue 1: V-Tip Wear at Contact Points

The V-prong tip is the thinnest metal protecting the most fragile part of your stone. On a marquise diamond, each pointed tip is its most vulnerable spot. The V-prong that covers it is the thinnest section of metal on the entire band.

Under normal daily wear, the V-tip experiences constant micro-abrasion from clothing, countertops, and adjacent prong contact. Metal wears at roughly 0.01–0.03mm per month at the contact point. At month 36, the average V-tip has thinned by 0.36–1.0mm.

At this threshold, the prong can no longer hold its shape under moderate impact. One knock on a hard surface can push the prong aside. The stone then has no tip protection and can chip or fall out.

**What to watch for:** Under a loupe, a worn V-tip looks bright and mirror-like on its inner face. Unworn metal looks slightly matte. Mirror-bright metal on any prong inner face means active wear is happening.

### Issue 2: Prong Alignment Drift Under Daily Torque

Every grip creates torsional force that twists your ring slightly around your finger. A solitaire absorbs this in its thick shank. An eternity band distributes it across every stone and prong at once.

Over months, this torsional stress pushes prongs away from their original set position. The drift is tiny — fractions of a millimeter — but it compounds. By year 3, prongs set at a 45° offset angle may be at 30°.

That 15° reduction closes the clearance between adjacent tips and accelerates contact frequency. More contact means faster wear. The Alternating V-Tip Principle depends on that angular clearance staying intact.

**The compounding problem:** As prongs thin from Issue 1, they flex more easily, speeding up drift. As prongs drift closer together, contact increases, speeding up thinning. By year 3, both processes run simultaneously.

### Issue 3: Stone Loosening from Tip-Pressure Imbalance

A marquise stone is held by four prongs: two at each pointed tip. Equal pressure at all four prongs keeps the stone centered. One prong tighter than the other creates a micro-tilt in the stone.

When a stone is micro-tilted, the overloaded tip's prong pushes harder against the girdle. Over time, the prong carves a micro-groove in the girdle surface. The stone loosens incrementally as the groove deepens.

This process runs silently for months before the stone wobbles visibly. When you finally feel it, the groove is already there — and the repair is more involved than a simple tightening.

**The at-home test:** Every 3 months, press gently on each stone — downward and sideways. Any movement at all means a prong is loose. Get it tightened before the groove deepens further.

### Issue 4: Metal Fatigue at the Prong-Shank Junction

In a solitaire, the prong base sits inside a thick basket of metal that braces it from all sides. In an eternity band, there's no basket. Each prong rises directly from the thin shank — typically 1.5–2.0mm wide and 1.2–1.5mm thick.

Under the repeated flex of daily wear, this junction experiences cyclic metal stress. Gold work-hardens under cyclic stress and develops micro-cracks at the prong base. These cracks are invisible to the naked eye but propagate steadily.

When a prong breaks at the shank junction, it breaks suddenly — without visible warning. You don't feel it coming. You just notice the stone is gone.

**Platinum vs 14k gold:** Platinum deforms before it breaks. A failing platinum prong bends visibly, giving you warning before the stone is lost. A 14k gold prong typically breaks without the bending stage. For heavy daily wear, platinum prongs significantly reduce the risk of sudden failure. See the [platinum marquise engagement ring guide](/platinum-marquise-engagement-ring) for a full metal comparison.

### Issue 5: V-Tip Retipping Requires Adjacent Stone Removal

When a round-prong eternity band needs retipping, a jeweler can access each bead individually. The round shape allows precise solder work without heat risk to adjacent stones. Most round-prong eternity band retipping costs $50–$100 per prong.

V-tip prong retipping on a marquise eternity band is more complex. The V-shape sits close to the adjacent stone. Applying torch heat near a diamond requires either full stone protection or stone removal.

In a marquise eternity band, protecting adjacent stones means removing them first. That process adds 2–3 hours of bench time per prong. A single V-tip retip costs $150–$350 depending on access difficulty.

**The full retipping bill:** If four prongs need retipping at year 3 — typical for a full eternity band — expect $600–$1,400 for the repair. The same work on a round-prong eternity band costs $200–$400. Factor this total cost of ownership into your purchase decision, not just the ring price.

