## The Year-3 Inspection Window

The Year-3 Inspection Window is the 6-month period between your ring's 2.5-year and 3-year mark. This is the most cost-effective maintenance window in the life of any marquise diamond eternity band.

During years 1–2, prong wear is below the threshold a jeweler can reliably measure without removing stones. The wear is happening, but there's nothing actionable yet. At year 3, wear is measurable — and still early enough to retip without removing adjacent stones.

By year 4, the V-tips have thinned to the point where the full stone-removal process is required, tripling the repair cost. The Year-3 Inspection Window is the last cheap repair you'll get.

**What a Year-3 Inspection includes:**

| Service | Cost | What this means |
|---------|------|-----------------|
| Prong thickness measurement under magnification | $0–$30 | Most jewelers include this free; sets the baseline for future visits |
| V-tip alignment check (alternating orientation) | Included in inspection | Verifies the Alternating V-Tip Principle is still holding; flags drift early |
| Stone press test (all four prongs per stone) | Included in inspection | Catches loose stones before the girdle groove stage begins |
| Metal fatigue check at prong-shank junction | $0–$50 | Worth every dollar; invisible cracks are found here |
| Proactive retipping if wear is found | $100–$200 | Only needed if measurable thinning is present |

**Verdict:** Total Year-3 Inspection with no issues: $80–$150. With proactive retipping: $180–$350. Compare that to a post-failure repair at year 4–5: $600–$1,400. Booking this inspection is the single highest-ROI action you can take as a marquise eternity band owner.

The [marquise diamond clarity guide](/marquise-diamond-clarity-guide) explains inclusions near stone tips — relevant because a tip inclusion makes a stone more vulnerable when prong pressure becomes uneven.

