## Eternity Band vs Half-Eternity: Which Survives Daily Wear Better

A full eternity band has stones around the complete ring circumference. A half-eternity (also called a half-hoop) has stones only across the top — roughly 60–70% of the band — with solid metal underneath.

For a marquise diamond band, this structural difference matters significantly. A full eternity band has no solid section to absorb flex forces. A half-eternity has a solid metal underside that acts as a stress buffer, absorbing the majority of grip-and-release forces before they reach the prongs.

| Feature | Full Eternity | Half-Eternity | What this means |
|---------|--------------|---------------|-----------------|
| Prong count | Full circumference | Top half only | Full eternity has roughly 2× the prongs to maintain |
| Annual retipping cost at year 3 | $400–$1,400 | $200–$700 | Half-eternity cuts long-term maintenance cost roughly in half |
| Resizing | Not possible | ±2 sizes | Half-eternity is the only option if your finger size may change |
| Visual impact | Maximum — all-around sparkle | High — sparkle at top, clean profile at side | Full eternity wins on flash; half-eternity wins on practicality |
| Flex absorption | None | Solid underside absorbs force | Half-eternity prongs last measurably longer under daily wear |

**Verdict:** If you wear your ring during exercise, cooking, or manual work, choose the half-eternity. You cut your 10-year maintenance cost by roughly 40–50%. If your wear is light — mostly office and social — the full eternity band's visual impact justifies the extra maintenance.

