## Matching Your Marquise Diamond Eternity Band to Your Engagement Ring

A marquise eternity band works best when it curves flush against your marquise engagement ring. Three factors determine the fit.

**Band width.** Most marquise engagement rings have a shank width of 1.8–2.2mm. Your eternity band should match this within 0.2mm. A narrower band sits below the engagement ring's profile and looks disconnected. A wider band hides the gallery and can cause prong-to-prong interference between the two rings.

**Metal type.** Mixing 14k and 18k yellow gold creates a visible color difference within 6 months. The 14k will look slightly more yellow. Mixing white gold with platinum also creates color drift over time. Stay within the same metal type and karatage. See the [white gold marquise engagement ring guide](/white-gold-marquise-engagement-ring) or [yellow gold marquise engagement ring guide](/yellow-gold-marquise-engagement-ring) for color-matching specifics.

**Stone orientation and clearance.** A classic marquise engagement ring has the stone set tip-to-tip along the finger. An eternity band with classic marquise stones set the same way will have its V-prongs pointing toward the engagement ring's gallery. This causes the two rings to catch against each other. The east-west eternity band solves this — the 90° rotation moves V-tips away from the engagement ring, not toward it. See the [east-west marquise diamond ring guide](/east-west-marquise-diamond-ring) for the full stacking breakdown.

