## How to Choose a Setter for a Marquise Diamond Eternity Band

The setter matters more for this band style than almost any other ring you can buy. The five failure patterns above are almost entirely preventable — but only if the initial setting is done correctly. A wrong V-prong orientation on even one stone starts the wear clock immediately.

Here's exactly what to ask before handing over your ring:

**"How do you orient the V-prongs on adjacent stones?"** The correct answer is "alternating" or "offset." The setter should describe pointing the tips away from each other. If they look uncertain or give a generic answer, that's a red flag.

**"Do you retip marquise V-prongs in-house?"** A jeweler who sends the work out adds shipping time and cost to every future repair. In-house retipping means faster turnaround, lower cost, and direct accountability. This question alone filters out the setters who rarely work with marquise eternity bands.

**"What gauge wire do you use for the V-prongs on an eternity band?"** This is an advanced question — most buyers don't ask it. But it separates a specialist from a generalist. A setter who knows their work will tell you the gauge immediately. The right answer for 14k gold is typically 0.8–1.0mm at the prong base, tapering to 0.3–0.5mm at the tip. Anything below 0.3mm at the tip is too thin for daily wear.

**What to do if you already bought the ring:** Ask your jeweler to do a baseline prong inspection before you start wearing it daily. They should document the V-prong orientation, tip thickness, and alignment. This creates a reference for every future check-in. If the original setter didn't alternate the prongs correctly, a qualified bench jeweler can re-tip in the corrected orientation — ideally before any wear has begun.

The [marquise solitaire engagement ring guide](/marquise-solitaire-engagement-ring) has a detailed breakdown of what to look for in a setter, including the specific questions that distinguish V-tip specialists from general bench jewelers.

