## FAQ

### What is a marquise diamond eternity band?

A marquise diamond eternity band is a ring with continuous marquise-cut diamonds set all the way around the band. Each stone has pointed tips requiring V-shaped prongs. This creates a distinctive elongated sparkle profile that differs from any other eternity band style.

### How long does a marquise diamond eternity band last?

A correctly set marquise diamond eternity band with regular maintenance lasts 15–20+ years. The key word is maintenance. V-tip prongs need inspection every 6 months. The Year-3 Inspection Window is the most critical service point in the band's long-term durability.

### Why do marquise eternity bands need more maintenance than round-prong bands?

V-tip prongs have a smaller metal cross-section than round beads and wear faster under micro-abrasion. Adjacent V-tips sit very close together in an eternity band. This creates metal-on-metal contact that round beads don't experience — requiring 6-month service intervals instead of 12-month.

### What is the Alternating V-Tip Principle?

The Alternating V-Tip Principle is the requirement that adjacent stones in a marquise eternity band have their V-prongs oriented in opposite directions. This offset prevents direct tip-to-tip contact. When prongs face each other directly, metal grinds on metal with every flex, thinning the tips to failure by years 3–5.

### Can a marquise diamond eternity band be resized?

A full eternity band cannot be resized. The continuous stone channel leaves no solid metal section to add or remove material. A half-eternity band can be resized by ±2 sizes because the solid metal underside allows material to be adjusted.

### How much does it cost to maintain a marquise diamond eternity band?

A Year-3 inspection costs $80–$150. Add $100–$200 for proactive retipping if wear is found. If you miss the Year-3 Window and wait until a prong fails, a single V-tip retip runs $150–$350. Four prongs runs $600–$1,400. Annual cleaning and check-ins in years 1–2 cost $50–$100.

### What is the difference between an eternity band and a half-eternity band?

A full eternity band has stones continuously around the entire ring. A half-eternity has stones only across the top half, with solid metal underneath. The half-eternity can be resized, absorbs flex forces better, and costs significantly less to maintain over time. The full eternity band delivers more visual impact.

### Is lab-grown or natural diamond better for a marquise eternity band?

Lab-grown is the better financial choice for daily wear. You save 60–75% at purchase and face identical prong maintenance costs — diamond hardness is the same regardless of origin. The resale value of lab-grown is lower, but for a band you plan to keep long-term, this rarely matters.

### What metal is best for a marquise diamond eternity band?

Platinum prongs provide the best long-term protection. Platinum deforms before it breaks, giving visible warning before a prong fails completely. 14k gold prongs typically break without warning. If budget is the priority, 14k yellow gold hides prong wear marks better than 14k white gold under daily conditions.

### How do I check if a prong on my marquise eternity band is loose?

Press gently on each stone — downward and sideways — every 3 months. Any movement means a prong is loose. Under a 10× loupe, a worn V-tip shows bright, mirror-polished metal on its inner face. Normal unworn metal looks slightly matte by comparison.

### What is the best marquise eternity band for ring stacking?

The east-west marquise eternity band is the best choice for stacking. The 90° stone rotation moves V-prong tips away from the engagement ring gallery, eliminating prong catching. It also reduces tip-to-tip contact between adjacent eternity stones — the most common prong wear pattern.

### Where can I see all marquise diamond eternity band options?

Browse the full certified market inventory at the [marquise diamond eternity ring collection](https://www.bluenile.com/jewelry?ComponentIDs=DiamondEssentials&stoneShape=marquise&diamondBasicsLabel=eternity_rings&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) and the [marquise wedding ring collection](https://www.bluenile.com/wedding-rings?stoneShape=marquise&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) for the full range of settings, metals, and carat weights currently in certified stock.

